  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH NEXT WEEK/...\nThe active weather pattern affecting mostly the northern tier \nstates will continue through the weekend into next week. After the\nweather system affects mostly the Great Basin on Friday ejects to\nthe east on Saturday, weak upper level ridging is favored to \neventually move over the Desert Southwest lasting through the \nrest of the weekend. The cooler air behind the system will \nhowever be hard to move out of the region. Below normal \ntemperatures are forecast for the weekend with Saturday's highs \nmostly in the upper 60s to right around 70 degrees Sunday. \nOvernight lows will also take a decent dip with lower desert \nreadings falling into the 40s. Many higher terrain areas should \nalso see lows well into the 30s this weekend with the coldest air \nand lows dipping below freezing across much of the northern \nArizona high country.\n\nModel spread increases enough for early next week to put some\ndoubt in what will happen with a potential weak disturbance that\nmay move through or near our region on Monday. At the moment, the\nGEFS somewhat favors some sort of a weak and mostly dry system \nmoving through, while the EPS favors an even faster and weaker\nshortwave bypassing us to the north but holding back a deeper\ntrough off the West Coast. No matter the eventual solution, very\nlittle moisture is expected to move through our region and this is\nreflected in the less than 10% PoPs over the lower deserts to at\nmost 10-20% for the higher terrain. NBM forecast temperatures also\nfavor steady-state readings, but if model trends favor the EPS we\nmay see temperatures rise back closer to 75 degrees for highs.\nLooking out even further, ensembles favor a more significant ridge\nbuilding along and off the West Coast later next week. If this\nholds true, we should see temperatures rise to above normal for \nthe Thanksgiving holiday weekend.",
